🔹 Main Filter Categories
1️⃣ Blur Filters

Used to soften or defocus areas.

Common ones:

Gaussian Blur → Smooth, soft blur

Motion Blur → Directional movement effect

Lens Blur → Realistic depth blur

Field Blur (Blur Gallery) → Controlled depth blur

Use cases:

Background blur

Depth of field simulation

Softening harsh details

2️⃣ Sharpen Filters

Increase edge contrast to enhance detail.

Common ones:

Unsharp Mask

Smart Sharpen

High Pass (advanced method)

Use cases:

Product images

Portrait clarity

Print preparation

3️⃣ Noise Filters

Add or reduce grain.

Add Noise

Reduce Noise

Dust & Scratches

Use cases:

Cleaning old images

Adding film grain

Sensor dust cleanup

4️⃣ Distort Filters

Change shape or geometry.

Liquify

Ripple

Twirl

Wave

Use cases:

Face/body adjustments

Creative effects

5️⃣ Stylize Filters

Artistic effects.

Oil Paint

Emboss

Find Edges

Glowing Edges

Use cases:

Graphic design effects

Posters

Creative edits

6️⃣ Camera Raw Filter (Most Powerful)

Acts like a mini Lightroom inside Photoshop.

Includes:

Exposure

Contrast

Color grading

Clarity

Texture

Dehaze

Masking tools

Professional photographers use this heavily.

🔥 Important Concept: Smart Filters

If you convert your layer to a Smart Object before applying filters:

Right-click layer → Convert to Smart Object → Apply Filter

Benefits:

Editable later

Non-destructive

Maskable

Professionals rarely apply filters destructively.

🔎 Filter vs Adjustment Layer
Filter Adjustment Layer
Alters pixels directly Adjusts tonal values
Often destructive Non-destructive
Blur, distort, stylize Exposure, color, contrast
⚡ Professional Workflow

Duplicate layer

Convert to Smart Object

Apply Filter

Adjust filter mask if needed
